.TH TANGSIBLE-VAULT 1 "August 2026" "Tangsible" "Tangsible Manual"
.SH NAME
tangsible-vault \- edit individually vault\-encrypted variables in place
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tangsible vault
.I filename
.RB [ \-\-vault\-password\-file
.I path
.RB | " \-\-ask\-vault\-pass" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B vault
provides an
.BR "ansible\-vault edit" \-like
experience, but scoped to individual variables instead of the whole file.
.BR ansible-vault (1)
itself only offers whole\-file encryption via
.B edit
/
.BR encrypt ,
which turns every change into a huge diff, or per\-variable encryption via
.BR encrypt_string ,
which means specifying secrets on the command line and copy\-pasting
ciphertext by hand. Neither is pleasant for a value you expect to change
more than once.
.PP
.I filename
is decrypted in place: every top\-level
.B !vault
\-tagged value becomes its plain decrypted string, opened in
.B $VISUAL
/
.B $EDITOR
(falling back to
.B vi
if neither is set) exactly like
.BR "git commit" 's
message editor. On save, only values that actually changed are
re\-encrypted; everything else \(em comments, key order, and any untouched
ciphertext \(em is spliced back byte\-for\-byte from the original file, not
regenerated. Vault's own cipher uses a fresh random salt on every
encryption, so re\-encrypting an untouched value would otherwise make its
ciphertext (and the diff) change for no reason on every single save.
This is a separate, standalone program from
.BR tangsible-run (1)
and friends: there is no tree here, and no TUI at all.
.PP
Only top\-level
.B key: value
entries are supported \(em the shape
.B "ansible\-vault encrypt_string"
already produces, and the only one any real vault file has ever actually
used in practice. A
.B !vault
value found nested inside a list or a mapping is rejected outright rather
than silently mishandled.
.SH WHAT GETS ENCRYPTED
.TP
An existing plaintext value
Left as plaintext when saving \(em
.B vault
never encrypts something on its own initiative \(em but a warning is
printed if it's a string, since that's the shape an accidentally
un\-vaulted secret would take.
.TP
A brand\-new value
Encrypted automatically, provided it's a plain string. A new value that
isn't a string (a list, a number, a boolean) can't be sensibly encrypted
and can't be silently left plaintext either, so it's treated the same as
the next case.
.TP
A previously\-encrypted value that no longer resolves to a string
A hard failure, not a silent fallback to plaintext: the file is not
written, and you're asked whether to fix it or revert (see THE EDITING
LOOP). The risk of a secret landing on disk unencrypted outweighs the
inconvenience.
.TP
A \fB!vault\fR tag typed or pasted by hand into the editor
Rejected the same way as any other unexpected
.B !vault
tag \(em the editable view only ever shows plain values, so this can only
mean something was typed in deliberately, and v1 doesn't support that.
.SH THE EDITING LOOP
If a save can't proceed as\-is \(em a value described above turned into
something that can't be encrypted, or the file no longer parses as valid
YAML at all \(em nothing is written to
.IR filename .
Instead,
.B vault
explains what's wrong (annotating the offending line with an inline
.B "# TANGSIBLE VAULT:"
comment, when there's a specific line to point at) and asks:
.RS
.EX
Fix this in the editor again, or revert and discard all changes? [f/r]
.EE
.RE
Answering
.B f
reopens the editor on the same content, annotation included, so nothing
already typed is lost. Answering
.B r
discards every change made in this session and exits; the target file was
never touched in the first place, so there is nothing to undo. This
prompt \(em not Ctrl\-C \(em is the supported way out of the loop: a
real full\-screen editor (vim, and similar) puts the terminal into raw
mode, which disables the terminal's own Ctrl\-C\-to\-SIGINT generation for
as long as it's running, so Ctrl\-C cannot reliably reach
.B tangsible
while the editor is actually open.
.SH PASSWORD RESOLUTION
The vault password is resolved in this order, most\-explicit\-wins:
.IP 1. 4
.B \-\-vault\-password\-file
.I path
.IP 2. 4
.B \-\-ask\-vault\-pass
(a no\-echo terminal prompt, right away)
.IP 3. 4
.B $ANSIBLE_VAULT_PASSWORD_FILE
.IP 4. 4
the project's own
.BR ansible.cfg ,
.B [defaults] vault_password_file
\(em found the same way
.B ansible
itself does:
.BR $ANSIBLE_CONFIG ,
then
.BR ./ansible.cfg ,
then
.BR ~/.ansible.cfg ,
then
.BR /etc/ansible/ansible.cfg
\(em since this is the standard, most common place a project already
configures its vault password
.IP 5. 4
.B .tangsible/config.toml's
.B [vault] password_file
(see FILES)
.IP 6. 4
an interactive no\-echo prompt, as a last resort
.PP
.B \-\-vault\-password\-file
and
.B \-\-ask\-vault\-pass
cannot be given together. Step 6 is a deliberate divergence from real
.BR ansible-vault ,
which errors instead of prompting when nothing is configured \(em accepted
here since editing one variable is inherently interactive, unlike a
.B ansible\-playbook
run.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The file was saved successfully, or the editor closed with no changes at
all (in which case nothing was written).
.TP
.B 1
A genuine failure: the file couldn't be read or decrypted (wrong
password, corrupted data), or some other I/O error.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error: no
.I filename
was given, or both password flags were given together.
.TP
.B 130
The session was reverted \(em either explicitly, via the fix\-or\-revert
prompt, or by Ctrl\-C reaching
.B tangsible
directly (only possible outside an active editor session: before it
first opens, or between reopen rounds). The target file is left exactly
as it was.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ANSIBLE_VAULT_PASSWORD_FILE
See PASSWORD RESOLUTION.
.TP
.B VISUAL, EDITOR
Consulted in that order to choose the editor. Falls back to
.B vi
if neither is set.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B ansible.cfg
Project's own Ansible configuration; see PASSWORD RESOLUTION.
Read\-only \(em never written by
.BR tangsible .
.TP
.B .tangsible/config.toml
Same project\-local, user\-authored, never\-written\-by\-Tangsible file
.BR tangsible (1)'s
CONFIGURATION section describes, with its own
.B [vault]
table:
.RS
.EX
[vault]
password_file = "./.vault_pass"
.EE
.RE
.SH LIMITATIONS
Multiple vault\-ids (Ansible's
.B $ANSIBLE_VAULT;1.2;AES256;label
form, letting one file mix ciphertexts under different passwords) are not
supported \(em a labeled vault header is rejected the same way an
unsupported shape anywhere else is, rather than silently mishandled.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tangsible (1),
.BR ansible-vault (1)
